{"study_accession":"PRJNA981458","secondary_study_accession":"SRP441811","title":"The transcriptome of HTLV-1-infected primary cells following reactivation reveals changes to host gene expression central to the proviral life cycle","description":"Infections by Human T cell Leukaemia Virus type 1 (HTLV-1) persist for the lifetime of the host by integrating into the genome of CD4+ T cells. Proviral gene expression is core to proviral survival and the maintenance of the proviral load, through the pro-proliferative changes it induces in infected cells. Despite their role in HTLV-1 infection and a persistent cytotoxic T lymphocyte response raised against them, proviral transcripts from the sense-strand are rarely detected in fresh cells extracted from the peripheral blood, and have recently been found to be expressed intermittently by a small subset of cells at a given time. Ex vivo culture of infected cells prompts synchronised proviral expression in infected cells from peripheral blood, allowing the study of factors involved in reactivation in primary cells. Here, we used bulk RNA-seq to examine the host transcriptome over six days in vitro, following proviral reactivation in primary peripheral CD4+ T cells isolated from subjects with non-malignant HTLV-1 infection. Infected cells displayed a conserved response to reactivation, characterised by discrete stages of gene expression, cell division and subsequently horizontal transmission of the virus. We observed widespread changes in Polycomb gene expression following reactivation, including an increase in PRC2 transcript levels and diverse changes in the expression of PRC1 components. We hypothesize that these transcriptional changes constitute a negative feedback loop that maintains proviral latency by re-deposition of H2AK119ub1 following the end of proviral expression. Using RNAi, we found that certain deubiquitinases, BAP1, USP14 and OTUD5 each promote proviral transcription. These data demonstrate the detailed trajectory of HTLV-1 proviral reactivation in primary HTLV-1-carrier lymphocytes and the impact on the host cell. Overall design: RNA-seq of CD4+ T lymphocytes collected from six HTLV-1-positive and three HTLV-1-negative volunteers and cultured ex vivo. Sampling at 0, 24, 48, 96, 120 and 144 hours.","pubmed":"37523412","geo_series":"","n_samples":54,"study_labels":[],"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN35675713","study_accession":"PRJNA981458","blood_class":"blood_cells","class_from":"sample","title":"TDX T144","description":"TDX T144","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM7468833","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2023-07-25","attributes":[["source_name","Blood"],["tissue","Blood"],["cell_type","CD4+ T cell"],["genotype","HTLV-1-infected"],["geo_loc_name","missing"],["collection_date","missing"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR24875538","experiment_accession":"SRX20639449","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina NovaSeq 6000","read_count":50445569,"base_count":15234561838,"first_public":"2023-07-25","experiment_title":"Illumina NovaSeq 6000 sequencing: GSM7468833: TDX T144 Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":false},{"sample_accession":"SAMN35675714","study_accession":"PRJNA981458","blood_class":"blood_cells","class_from":"sample","title":"TDX T120","description":"TDX T120","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM7468832","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2023-07-25","attributes":[["source_name","Blood"],["tissue","Blood"],["cell_type","CD4+ T cell"],["genotype","HTLV-1-infected"],["geo_loc_name","missing"],["collection_date","missing"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR24875539","experiment_accession":"SRX20639448","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina NovaSeq 6000","read_count":56906576,"base_count":17185785952,"first_public":"2023-07-25","experiment_title":"Illumina NovaSeq 6000 sequencing: GSM7468832: TDX T120 Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":f